tickets.defect:
`ProcessAction` gates EXECUTION on mode: `IsActingMode()` is false in `shadow`, so a breach's flatten is logged as `SHADOW_ACTION` and never sent. That is the entire promise of the mode, and the `MinShadowSessions` gate exists to make watching-before-acting the default.

But ten rule paths set `stateModel.IsLockedOut = true` BEFORE the action is dispatched, outside any mode check, and `RiskGuardAddOn.cs:112` `CanTrade` reads that flag ABOVE its own escape hatch:

    if (_accountStates.TryGetValue(accountName, out var state) && state.IsLockedOut)
    {
        bool bypassAllowed = !_isArmed && _config.LockoutBypassWhileDisarmedAccounts...
        if (!bypassAllowed) return false;
    }
    if (!_isArmed) return true;          // <- the arming escape hatch is BELOW the lockout

So in `shadow`: nothing is flattened, and the account **stops being allowed to trade**. `CanTrade` is the universal pre-trade gate -- the copier calls `CanTrade(followerName, instrument, "TradeCopier")` and every strategy calls it through `RiskManagerBase` -- so a shadow breach silently halts a bot. Its three refusal paths log to `NinjaTrader.Code.Output.Process` only, which no readable sink captures (`P1-71`), so nothing says why.

The missing information is the AUTHORITY under which a lockout was imposed. `IsLockedOut` records THAT a breach happened; nothing records whether the guard was in a position to act on it, so the consumer cannot tell an enforced lockout from an observation.

This became load-bearing when `F-9` mapped five accounts to firm plans, arming two more lockout-capable rules: an `Apex-100K` trailing breach needs an 1800 drop from peak, which is an ordinary week for an ORB strategy.
